17592186045417] [17592186110778 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110778 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110778 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110778 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1045 "Classifying Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cceda887-69b2-551b-ab27-e3d019c77f5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1061 36207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eab37537-55a4-56e5-9fd6-7ee823f295bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1061 9452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04c2712b-f67a-5317-b34e-8936b6acdd9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1061 9454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f398499-a037-5d65-8f25-ecbcfa7534c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1061 9459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14f9869d-2b87-58dd-a3bd-3dfd6047425b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1061 9460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "615b803e-ca7c-56d1-9167-9829ed84b5c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1064 "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other. \nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a  trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of parallel sides.\n\n \n\n![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png)\n\n Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent sides or  ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.\n\n \n\n![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png)\n\n A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n A rhombus where all angles are congruent is a **square**.\n\n \n\n![](images/94/941872050d5407bcd9cb532e318276adccff802230ed854a2d292666fc0096fc.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1065 2262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06a11fa2-5911-52f5-9a6d-1028e49b44dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1064 "We can organize all mentioned types of quadrilaterals into the following hierarchy diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nEach shape below another is a specific type of the one above it. For example, every square is a rectangle, and every rectangle is a parallelogram. This means every square is also a parallelogram." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1065 5931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d9d3be2f-753b-5a4b-9f26-20b34ab8f89a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying a Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6b66305f-f020-5236-a344-502bc54f8cc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing Polynomials in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "af3f4c35-be23-5a1a-97fb-18e89ab51591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying and Writing Polynomials in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6f00d5b6-5c41-5499-a897-b3ea7cbb3432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1045 "Simplifying Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "af23758d-7476-5d58-8b4e-e0d2c3022048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120613 #edb/ref 1061 150900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8789d5b3-8f11-513e-a769-fad4e067d79a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120614 #edb/ref 1061 150901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2339f28a-1bfe-5d7f-a1d4-4457e0b9dd39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120615 #edb/ref 1061 150904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab63d222-6bc9-5825-a10a-531c4978485f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120616 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120616 #edb/ref 1061 150905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120616 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "357f8d99-13be-51ca-a9b3-f2b34dc03d58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120616 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120617 #edb/ref 1061 150908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120617 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8288291c-78f5-5a1b-99f9-23e99b20eada" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120617 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121146 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify the polynomial below because it contains a pair of like terms:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{3x^2}} +4x \\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,{\\color{blue}{2x^2}} +5\n$$\n\nTo simplify, we combine the like terms ($3x^2$ and $-2x^2$) by adding or subtracting their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4x-2{x}^{2}+5 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+4x+5 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2})+4x+5 & = \\\\ (3-2){x}^{2}+4x+5 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121146 #edb/ref 1065 8864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121146 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dcac1959-d802-5641-8736-12d53db273a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121146 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121147 #edb/ref 1064 "To write a polynomial in **standard form**, we must arrange the terms of the variable's exponent in descending order. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nFor example, consider the following polynomial:\n\n$$\n6x-2x^2-2+x^3\n$$\n\nTo write this polynomial in standard form, we follow the steps below:\n\n1\\. Identify the term with the highest exponent ($x^3$) and write it first.\n\n2\\. Identify the term with the next highest exponent ($-2x^2$) and write it second.\n\n3\\. Continue identifying the term with the next highest exponent and writing it next until all of the polynomial terms have been written.\n\nTherefore, the standard form of our polynomial is\n\n$$\nx^3 -2x^2 + 6x -2.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The leading term always appears first when a polynomial is written in standard form. This is where the leading term gets its name!\n\n- When a polynomial is written in standard form, we also say that the terms are written in **decreasing powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** or **descending powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** because the powers are decreasing/descending.\n\n- If we were to write the terms of our polynomial in *increasing* (or *ascending*) powers of $x,$ it would look as follows:\n\n$$\n-2 + 6x -2x^2 + x^3\n$$\n\nIn this case, the powers of $x$ *increase* as we read from left to right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121147 #edb/ref 1065 8868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121147 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40e8c8a9-715e-5a33-92e4-34369c27b505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121147 #edb/ref 1067 "Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124481 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124481 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124481 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124481 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124481 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124481 #edb/ref 1039 "Separating a Square Root Into a Product of Two Square Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124481 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124481 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "202575c6-7672-5387-a5ae-762bef520add" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124482 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124482 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124482 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124482 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124482 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing a Product of Two Square Roots as a Single Square Root" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124482 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124482 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d6f8c2c9-e82f-5d37-9f34-4811dd88c78d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124483 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124483 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124483 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124483 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124483 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124483 #edb/ref 1039 "The Product Rule With Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124483 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124483 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e97098b0-a7f9-564a-899e-60fb88682c80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1045 "The Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124720 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "391e2b89-8cde-5a13-8210-4ec865147d71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125338 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125338 #edb/ref 1061 17274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125338 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ba8ca19-5df4-53c5-ab7a-0a7ba56e0bc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125338 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125339 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125339 #edb/ref 1061 17275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125339 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec31ad60-33b1-5f33-bc69-7dda09da6514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125339 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125340 #edb/ref 1061 17346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125340 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48f71bf2-cfc9-583f-81af-bf3c85f247e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125340 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125365 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125365 #edb/ref 1061 18176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125365 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dcc53fd7-b344-545d-8cd9-408d76d4ce85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125365 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125366 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125366 #edb/ref 1061 18178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125366 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85a7257b-e917-5ef4-854d-85d0f5b7753b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125366 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125367 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125367 #edb/ref 1061 18179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125367 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9b6616d-b6fe-5529-bc7e-89b4bc69c83e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125367 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125769 #edb/ref 1064 "To multiply square roots, we simply multiply the numbers under the root. This is called the **product rule for radicals**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}$ by applying the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nThe product rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5} = \\sqrt[3]{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[3]{10}.\n$$\n\nIn general, the product rule works for any product of $n$th roots. So for any $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{2} \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{5} = \\sqrt[n]{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[n]{10}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot use the product rule when we have the product of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression $\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt[3]{5}$ cannot be simplified because the radicals $\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$ are different." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125769 #edb/ref 1065 3296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125769 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f7f60ab3-2c97-5570-bacc-49ef3d371c52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125769 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125770 #edb/ref 1064 "The product rule can often be used to simplify square roots.\n\nFor example, consider $\\sqrt{98}.$ Note that $98=49 \\cdot 2,$ where $49$ is a perfect square. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{98} = \\sqrt{49 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{49} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = 7 \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nAlternatively, if we're given a simplified square root and we want to convert it to the square root of a single number, then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n7 \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{49} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{49 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{98}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125770 #edb/ref 1065 3298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125770 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6b8006c0-4a18-5c9d-84c6-1224cf85a921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125770 #edb/ref 1067 "Simplifying Square Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125771 #edb/ref 1064 "The product rule applies to cube roots as well.\n\nFor example, consider $\\sqrt[3]{24}.$ Note that $24=8 \\cdot 3,$ where $8$ is a perfect cube. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{24} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = 2 \\sqrt[3]{3}.\n$$\n\nAgain, if we're given a simplified cube root and we want to convert it to the cube root of a single number then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n2 \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{24}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125771 #edb/ref 1065 3299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125771 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d8fc754e-e350-5273-9188-9203a384b9b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125771 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule With Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129110 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129110 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Power of Product Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129110 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129110 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8aab5653-c3ec-5b4e-b56f-53393955d641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129111 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129111 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129111 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129111 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129111 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Power of Product Rule to Expressions with Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129111 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129111 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "525486fb-d976-57a5-b6bd-c33f19177a3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129112 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129112 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129112 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129112 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Power of Product Rule to Expressions with Negative Numbers"